**(a) Missing/partial requirements**

None of the diff-reviewable acceptance criteria are missing. The import criterion — "The root instruction file imports the agent-configuration file, so it is no longer reachable only by an explicit read" — is met via the literal `@AGENTS.md` import (line 10). The inlining criterion — "A trimmed invariant set is inlined as literal text, not imported" — is met: the five bullets (lines 19–34) are plain markdown, no `@` syntax. Length: the inlined block is ~23 lines vs. 144 in `.sandcastle/efficiency-rules.md`, satisfying "materially shorter than the full rules file." The three runtime-verification criteria are correctly out of scope per the task instructions.

**(b) Scope creep**

None found. The diff touches only `CLAUDE.md`, and every added line (import, explanatory framing, five bullets, pointer back to the rules file) serves one of the stated acceptance criteria. No unrelated edits.

**(c) Implemented but questionable**

The spec's bar for inclusion is narrow: "Keep the inlined set to the judgement-level invariants that no deterministic guard could express." Two of the five bullets sit close to mechanical rules already codified in `.sandcastle/efficiency-rules.md` §4, which weakens the case that they needed judgement-only inlining rather than being left as guard-expressible:

- "Don't duplicate a sub-agent's work. Once you've delegated a task to a sub-agent, wait for its report instead of independently re-doing the same analysis yourself" closely restates efficiency-rules.md's "Do not duplicate sub-agent work in the parent. Once you spawn review agents, stop reading the diff yourself" — a condition a guard could flag mechanically (parent re-running `git diff`/`Read` on the target after spawning a sub-agent).
- "Scope what you hand a sub-agent to what it actually needs... Don't pass full instruction files or unrelated context as boilerplate" restates "Feed sub-agents only what they need. Do not `cat AGENTS.md && cat CLAUDE.md` as boilerplate" — also plausibly guard-detectable (prompt contains verbatim contents of a known instructions file).

The other three bullets (trust prior documented investigation, fix the setup not the symptom, defect-vs-judgement in review) are genuinely judgement calls with no obvious mechanical check, matching the spec's intent well.

This isn't a hard defect — reasonable people could disagree on where "deterministic guard could express this" starts — so per the repo's own invariant ("a defect blocks; a difference of judgement doesn't"), I'd flag it as a nit rather than a blocker. No content in the inlined set contradicts `.sandcastle/efficiency-rules.md`; all five bullets are consistent generalizations of specific rules already there.Both review axes are complete. Let me do a final check that nothing was modified before reporting.
